From: Stained Glass OS contributors
Subject: server: a grant to the machine's users reaches them

In the shared machine prefix the users of the computer are the prefix's Unix
group (sgwine). Wine mapped a DACL's allow ACEs for Everyone, the owner and
the caller's own groups to mode bits and ignored the rest -- so a grant to
BUILTIN\Users, Authenticated Users or Interactive did nothing, and the file
kept its maker's group. Steam's installer, run as an administrator, gives
the users its folder; Steam, run later by a user, stopped at once:
"Directory C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\package not writable".

Such a grant is now the group's bits, and when it gives write, the server
(for what its own account owns) puts the file in the users' group and a
directory setgid, so what is made in it later is the group's too -- and
passes the grant down to what the directory already holds, as
SetNamedSecurityInfo passes inheritable ACEs on. SG_USERS_GROUP names the
group for gates (default sgwine).

Gate: test/usersacl-gate.sh (make test-usersacl).
---
--- a/server/file.c
+++ b/server/file.c
@@ -28,6 +28,8 @@
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
+#include <grp.h>
+#include <ftw.h>
 #include <sys/time.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <time.h>
@@ -437,6 +439,72 @@
